00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.800
Now in production,

192
00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:33.200
the MCP kind of gives us a couple of challenges.

193
00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:38.080
And the main challenge is comes back from that initial design, right?

194
00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.640
Which is that if I have a kind of a client and servant pairing,

195
00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:45.680
there's an assumption in the protocol that the

196
00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:50.320
the relationship between them is stateful.

197
00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.840
So what's actually happening here is, is that the client will make a request

198
00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:01.760
to the server, and the server says I've initialized.

199
00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.400
And then the client sends back a notification and they've kind of hand

200
00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:06.200
shaken.

201
00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:08.280
So we've already kind of two messages from the client

202
00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.280
to the server in before we've actually established a session.

203
00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.200
After that, the client then will normally check and say, right,

204
00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:16.800
well give me the list of tools,

205
00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:18.760
give me the list of prompts, give me the list of resources.

206
00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:22.720
We're so we're now kind of we're at four calls in before we've actually even

207
00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:24.120
we've actually even serviced anything.

208
00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:25.880
And then finally we'll get a tool call

209
00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.160
in the information.

210
00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.800
We'll go back to the client.

211
00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.800
Now again kind of typically

212
00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:38.080
what what we'll see in these kind of scenarios is if we've got state

213
00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:41.080
at both the client and the server, that starts to get rather

214
00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.400
rather tricky to manage.

215
00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.360
And it's also very, very chatty.

216
00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:52.080
So I kind of ran this I think it was on Sunday morning.

217
00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:56.760
This number kind of varies a bit based on the mix of clients we've recently seen.

218
00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:00.440
But at the moment, for every 10 million protocol messages that we get.

219
00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:04.520
So these are the kind of MCP protocol methods and calls, 1.2

220
00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:09.080
million of them are initialize events, which can may be interesting,

221
00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:11.960
but actually only 62,000 of them at all calls.

222
00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:16.880
So the kind of overhead of managing that stateful connection

223
00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:21.440
is, is quite high from from a protocol level.

224
00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:26.680
Now there is a kind of semi documented

225
00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:29.880
undocumented mode where you can kind of run MCP stateless.

226
00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.000
It's a bit ambiguous, but if you do that,

227
00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.040
you lose any ability to kind of capture analytics.

228
00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.560
So some of the challenges then we have with with stateful.

229
00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.640
So one is that ambiguity around, around the protocol,

230
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.720
the use of elicitation and sampling, if you've got server

231
00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:52.200
initiated requests, mean that you need to maintain an open channel.

232
00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:52.480
Okay.

233
00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.960
So one of the things that we were quite keen on trying when we first

234
00:12:55.960 --> 00:13:00.640
launched was to send what are called tool list change notifications.

235
00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.120
So the MCP server, if it wants to offer different tools, will notify the client.

236
00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:05.440
And the client can then offer them.

237
00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:07.960
But actually in practice, that's a very, very difficult thing to do,

238
00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:10.080
especially on a public server.

239
00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:13.840
As I mentioned, session state and understanding

240
00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.120
that is not quite precisely defined enough in the protocol.

241
00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.200
So a standard IO server, you kind of just connect,

242
00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.600
you've got a process and you talk to it a remote server,

243
00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:29.160
you have an MCP session ID, it doesn't necessarily match up

244
00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.440
an operationally having sticky sessions and load balancers,

245
00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:34.880
which need to inspect the traffic

246
00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.960
to send it to the right server, means that doing server upgrades, resilience,

247
00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:44.360
or kind of standard scaling practice becomes very, very difficult.

248
00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:48.080
The other challenge as well is SSE connection

249
00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:52.200
